# Inter-VLAN Routing – Router-on-a-Stick Lab

### Cisco Switching and Routing | VLAN 10, VLAN 20 & VLAN 40

## 1. Project Overview

This lab demonstrates how to configure Inter-VLAN Routing using the **Router-on-a-Stick (ROAS)** technique.

The objective is to allow devices belonging to different VLANs and IP subnets to communicate through router subinterfaces using IEEE 802.1Q trunking.

The lab uses:

- Cisco Layer 2 switches
- Cisco routers
- VLAN segmentation
- 802.1Q trunking
- Router subinterfaces
- Inter-VLAN routing
- End-to-end connectivity testing

## 2. Network Topology

The lab contains two separate Router-on-a-Stick configurations.

**Network 1 – R1 and SW1**

- VLAN 10 – HR
- VLAN 20 – Engineering

**Network 2 – R2 and its access switch**

- VLAN 40 – London

Each router provides the default gateway for its connected VLAN.

