# 12. Understanding How Router-on-a-Stick Works

Router-on-a-Stick allows multiple VLANs to communicate using one physical router interface.

The process works as follows:

1. A PC sends traffic to another VLAN.
2. The switch forwards the traffic through its trunk port.
3. The trunk carries the frame with an 802.1Q VLAN tag.
4. The router receives the tagged frame through the physical interface.
5. The matching router subinterface processes the VLAN traffic.
6. The router performs Layer 3 routing.
7. The packet is forwarded back through the trunk with the appropriate VLAN tag.
8. The switch delivers the traffic to the destination PC.

