## 14. Troubleshooting Guide

| Problem                  | Possible Solution                         |
| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------- |
| PC cannot reach gateway  | Check PC IP, gateway and router interface |
| VLAN-to-VLAN ping fails  | Check router subinterfaces and dot1q tags |
| Trunk is not working     | Check trunk mode and allowed VLANs        |
| VLAN is missing          | Create the VLAN on the switch             |
| Router interface is down | Use `no shutdown`                         |
| PC cannot communicate    | Check access port VLAN assignment         |
| PC4 cannot reach gateway | Check VLAN 40 and R2 trunk configuration  |

## 15. Important Lab Notes

- The switch-to-router connection must operate as an 802.1Q trunk.
- Each VLAN requires its own router subinterface.
- Every VLAN must have a unique IP subnet.
- Each PC must use the correct default gateway.
- Router physical interfaces must be enabled.
- Access ports must be assigned to the correct VLAN.
- The trunk must allow the VLAN IDs required by the lab.

**Routing limitation:** Network 1 (R1) and Network 2 (R2) are separate networks in this lab. PC4 will not automatically communicate with PC1, PC2 or PC3. Communication between the two router networks requires an additional routed connection between R1 and R2 and appropriate routing configuration.

